    "passage": "An important part of maintaining a healthy garden is to ensure that good amounts of nutrients are in the soil. Plants use the nutrients in the soil for many of their basic processes, so the soil should be fertilized properly. Nitrogen is a chemical that helps plants to absorb light and turn it into energy. Phosphorous is another to [[1]] energy within an organism to other parts, such as to the stems, the leaves or the roots. They all play important roles in guaranteeing that plants [[2]] healthy flowers, fruits and vegetables. When applying nitrogen, gardeners should be careful. With an adequate amount, it helps plants boost energy, but [[3]] can negatively influence the growth of plants and vegetables. Besides, gardens can also [[4]] phosphorous, which can maximize the rate of plant growth and maturation. However, farmers near bodies of water should be careful when applying phosphorous. [[5]] it is unlikely to harm plants with too much of it, excessive amounts of phosphorous flowing into the water may pollute and impact the ecosystem. Many fertilizers have a combination of the two chemicals, or they can be applied separately to make gardens grow healthy.",
